# Reduction of the USC-II Combustion Mechanism

**Author:** autonomous research run (principal `a712b8bd`)
**Date:** 2026-09-16
**Parent mechanism:** USC Mech II (Wang et al., *Combust. Flame* 2007),
111 species / 784 reactions, Cantera YAML (`data/parent_usc_ii.yaml`,
sha256 `eefd846bf67253ecf8a35c99bf2387b9a38b3f69a94640c013d99b0f16277aee`)

## 1. Summary

The USC-II mechanism (111 species / 784 reactions) was reduced with a
self-built DRG (directed relation graph) workflow. Products were validated
against the parent on two experimentally measurable macroscopic observables
— autoignition delay time (IDT) and laminar flame speed (SL) — over 9
chemically diverse fuels (H2, CO, CH4, CH3OH, C2H2, C2H4, C2H6, C3H8,
nC4H10), φ = 0.5–1.5, P = 1–30 atm, T0 = 1000–2000 K (IDT; 891 cases) and
φ = 0.6–1.6, Tu = 300–400 K, P = 1–5 atm (SL; 216 cases).

Three reduced mechanisms are delivered, all passing explicit consistency
checks (element-balanced reactions, verbatim parent thermo/transport for
retained species, no orphan species, no dangling third-body efficiencies):

| tier | file | species | reactions | IDT mean / max err | SL mean / max err |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| conservative (L) | `mechs/usc2_reduced_L.yaml` | 97 (−12.6 %) | 712 (−9.2 %) | 0.24 % / 5.2 % | 0.13 % / 0.88 % |
| balanced (M) | `mechs/usc2_reduced_M.yaml` | 88 (−20.7 %) | 646 (−17.6 %) | 0.40 % / 5.8 % | 0.28 % / 1.7 % |
| aggressive (S) | `mechs/usc2_reduced_S.yaml` | 80 (−28.0 %) | 566 (−27.8 %) | 0.79 % / 9.3 % | 0.65 % / 6.0 % |

All three tiers satisfy the a-priori tolerance (mean ≤ 5 %, max ≤ 20 % vs
parent) at every one of the 1107 validation conditions.

The full accuracy/complexity trade-off over 16 candidate mechanisms
(27–97 species) is in `results/tradeoff_table.json` and
`results/tradeoff.png`. The useful-accuracy boundary for this validation
breadth lies near ~75–80 species: below it, low-temperature n-alkane
ignition degrades rapidly (§4.1, §5.2).

## 2. Provenance, scope, and consistency

- Parent = USC Mech II (111 sp / 784 rxn; header: `cti2yaml` from
  `USC_Mech_II.cti`, Cantera 3.1.0). **No downloaded or third-party
  mechanism was used**; every delivered mechanism was derived from the
  parent by our own code in this workspace (`src/`).
- Retained species keep the parent's NASA-7 thermo, transport and elemental
  composition verbatim (verified by `src/check_consistency.py`); only
  whole, element-balanced reactions are removed, so element conservation
  and thermodynamic consistency hold by construction *and* were verified
  explicitly. All three **delivered** mechanisms: 0 issues. Across all 50
  generated candidates, exactly one rejected aggressive candidate
  (usc2pf_full_k15, not delivered) was flagged for a benign orphan species
  (l-C6H4, retained with no remaining reaction).
- The parent's known minor NASA-7 fit discontinuities (Cantera
  `NasaPoly2::validate` warnings for CH2OCH, C4H5-2, C4H6-2, C2H3CHOCH2,
  CH3CHCHCHO) are inherited unchanged **deliberately**, keeping overlapping
  predictions bit-consistent with the parent.
- Third-body efficiency maps are pruned of removed species; the parent's
  duplicate-reaction flags are preserved.

## 3. Methods

### 3.1 Toolchain (built for this run)

- Cantera 3.1.0 + NumPy/SciPy under Python 3.12; a 64-core remote node ran
  sweeps/validation (offline wheels in `wheels/`, bootstrap
  `src/remote_bootstrap.sh`).
- `src/drg.py`: state sampling (0-D adiabatic const-P ignition trajectories
  + 1-D freely-propagating flames); DRG coefficients
  r_AB = max_i |ν_Ai ω_i δ_Bi| / max(P_A, C_A) with
  P_A/C_A the total production/consumption rates of A;
  R_AB = max over sampled states; species selection by single-hop
  threshold, iterative DFS, or DRGEP max-product paths; reaction importance
  I_i = max over states and species of |ν_Ai ω_i| / max(P_A, C_A).
- `src/reduce.py` (global selection + top-K ladder),
  `src/reduce_perfuel.py` (per-fuel R matrices + union selection),
  `src/repair.py` (greedy single-species add-back at worst conditions),
  `src/validate.py` (parallel IDT/SL runners, per-case timeouts),
  `src/compare.py`, `src/check_consistency.py`, `src/tradeoff.py`.

### 3.2 DRG state sampling

513 cases → 18 257 states (`results/states_full/`): 9 fuels × φ{0.5,1,1.5}
× P{1,10,30 atm} × T0{1000…1800 K} ignition trajectories (405) + 9 fuels ×
φ{0.8,1,1.4} × Tu{300,400 K} × P{1,5 atm} flames (108); states log-spaced
along each trajectory/flame.

### 3.3 What worked and what did not (incl. negative results)

1. **DRGEP max-product path search saturates** on this multi-fuel,
   wide-condition ensemble: the max-accumulated R matrix is dense (~17 % of
   species pairs have R > 0.1), so DRGEP retains ~108/110 species for every
   eps ∈ [1e-4, 0.3]; iterative DFS graph search saturates identically
   (110/110). → selection by threshold/rank instead of graph search.
2. **Targeting hub radicals (H/O/OH/HO2) over-anchors** the graph; final
   DRG targets are only the fuel species + O2.
3. **Global all-fuels max-accumulation inflates importance**: with 18 257
   states, ~91 species tie at importance ≈ 1.0, making small top-K cuts
   arbitrary. **Per-fuel engines + union** (one R matrix per fuel from that
   fuel's states only) restore discrimination — used for the M and S tiers.
4. **Greedy single-hop ranking has a multi-hop blind spot**: it dropped
   1-butene (C4H81), a two-hop intermediate of nC4H10 low-T oxidation
   (sC4H9 → C4H8 + HO2), causing +22 % low-T nC4H10 IDT error at 77
   species. A worst-condition single-species add-back scan (34 candidates)
   identified C4H81 as the unique fix (error → −2 %).
5. **A myopic repair fails**: an early top75+C4H81 mechanism (78 sp)
   passed its nC4H10-only repair conditions at 4.3 % but is catastrophically
   wrong for C2H2 low-T ignition on the full grid (max +584 % at 1100 K —
   C2H2 was neither in the repair conditions nor in the screening
   mini-grid). → rejected. The final S tier instead repairs the per-fuel
   k40 mechanism with C2H2-covered conditions, adding **HCCOH** (the
   C2H2-oxidation bottleneck): full-grid max IDT error 22.7 % → 9.3 %,
   mean 1.15 % → 0.79 %. Lesson recorded: screening/repair condition sets
   must cover every fuel in the validation envelope, and any repaired
   candidate must be re-validated on the full grid before adoption.
6. **Reaction-level pruning beyond species-driven removal was not applied**:
   across the multi-fuel ensemble only ~5/784 reactions have importance
   < 0.01 — the reaction graph is as dense as the species graph, so
   reaction counts naturally track species counts.

### 3.4 Validation protocol

- Reference = **parent mechanism predictions** (not experiment); the parent
  was validated by its authors against shock-tube and flame data
  (Wang et al. 2007). Experimental context is a spot check (§6).
- IDT: adiabatic constant-pressure 0-D reactor, IDT = time of max dT/dt,
  20 s physical cap; all 891 parent cases ignited (0 failures).
- SL: freely-propagating 1-D flame, mixture-averaged transport, adaptive
  grid; all 216 parent cases converged (0 failures).
- Full grid (per mechanism): IDT 9 fuels × φ{0.5,1,1.5} × P{1,10,30 atm} ×
  11 T0 (1000–2000 K) = 891; SL 9 fuels × φ{0.6,0.8,1,1.2,1.4,1.6} ×
  Tu{300,400 K} × P{1,5 atm} = 216.
- Screening mini-grid (5 fuels: H2, CO, CH4, C2H4, nC4H10; 285 cases) for
  the 14-mechanism ladder.
- A-priori tolerances for "useful accuracy": mean |err| ≤ 5 % and
  max |err| ≤ 20 % vs parent. All three delivered tiers satisfy this at
  every validation condition (see §4.2).

