### 4.4 Where the errors live

- L/M tiers: worst fuel is C2H2 at φ = 1.5, 10–30 atm (rich, high-pressure
  corner; max 5.2–5.8 %) — the envelope's edge where acetylene
  polymerization/aromatics-initiation chemistry matters.
- S tier: worst fuels are C2H2 (max 9.3 %, lean low-T ignition) and
  nC4H10 (8.4 %, low-T) for IDT, C3H8 (6.0 %) for SL — after the
  HCCOH add-back removed the single 22.7 % C2H2 outlier of the
  unrepaired 79-species k40 mechanism.
- Small fuels (H2, CO, CH4, CH3OH) remain within ≲ 1.5 % at all tiers.

## 5. Discussion

### 5.1 The trade-off, quantitatively

Across 16 candidates: species 111 → 97 costs nothing measurable
(max err < 0.2 % IDT on mini-grid; 5.2 % on the harder full grid);
111 → 88 costs ≲ 1 % mean; 111 → 80 (with a one-species HCCOH repair)
costs ~0.8 % mean with worst-corner errors ≲ 9 %; below ~75 species the
price rises superlinearly (mean IDT error doubles at ~70, triples at ~65;
> 100 % below 60).
Reaction counts track species counts (712/646/566 for the three tiers).

### 5.2 Why the reduction saturates around ~80 species

USC-II = H2/CO/C1–C4 core chemistry + a C5–C6 aromatics block (~25
species) + iso-C4 branch. The aromatics and iso-C4 are the only sizeable
weakly-coupled groups; the C1–C4 core is strongly coupled through the
H/OH/C1–C4 radical pool across the chemically diverse validation set
(9 fuels × wide T/P/φ). A mechanism that must stay accurate for *all nine
fuels simultaneously* therefore keeps most of the core; below ~75 species
some fuel's chain necessarily breaks (first nC4H10 low-T, then C3/C4
generally, then rich C2H2). Much smaller mechanisms are possible only for
narrower envelopes (e.g., H2/CO/CH4 only) — outside this run's chosen
scope of maximum chemical diversity.

### 5.3 Limitations

- Validation is against the parent, not raw experiment; any parent-model
  bias propagates to all tiers. §6 is a spot check, not a systematic
  experimental validation.
- The validation grid is finite: no Tu > 400 K, P > 30 atm, flames beyond
  φ 0.6–1.6, no counterflow/extinction limits, no flame-structure or
  species-profile targets, no low-temperature (< 1000 K) NTC regime.
- Greedy importance selection (+ optional 1-species repair) is not globally
  optimal; per-application bespoke mechanisms can be smaller.
- IDT definition (max dT/dt, constant-P) differs from some experimental
  conventions (e.g., OH* onset); parent-vs-reduced comparisons are
  internally consistent, which is what the reduction task requires.

## 6. Experimental context (literature values — clearly not our data)

Parent predictions at canonical conditions (and hence the L/M tiers, which
match it to ≲ 1 %) fall inside commonly reported experimental ranges:
- CH4/air, φ = 1, 300 K, 1 atm: parent SL = **36.5 cm/s** vs ~36 ± 4 cm/s
  reported (e.g., Vagelopoulos & Egolfopoulos, *Combust. Flame* 1998).
- C3H8/air and nC4H10/air, φ = 1, 300 K, 1 atm: parent SL = **39.6 / 40.0
  cm/s** vs typical reported ~38–40 cm/s.
- H2/air, φ = 1.8 (near peak), 300 K, 1 atm: parent SL = **295.8 cm/s**;
  literature spans roughly 2–3 m/s there (large stretch-related scatter).
- For systematic parent-vs-experiment validation see Wang et al. 2007 (the
  parent's own validation against shock-tube IDT and flame speeds for
  H2/CO/C1–C4 fuels).

